-- The first program the rewritten resolve stage compiles end to end - Analysis-IR.md part 11.4,
-- milestone 1. A module-level constant, and a `main` that hands it back.
--
-- Deliberately free of everything: no import, no prelude name, no `Int`. `compiler/resolve_mir` does
-- not build Core yet, and the width names are builtin rather than declared - `Int` and `Long` are
-- Core aliases over `I32` and `I64`, so a fixture that writes the width directly is the same program
-- to both stages and needs a standard library from neither.
--
-- It is an ordinary fixture as well: the current stage compiles it, and the `.run.expect` beside it
-- is what *both* are checked against. Two implementations of one language answering differently is
-- the thing the second set of goldens exists to catch, so there is deliberately only one expected
-- answer.

let answer = 42 :: I32

fn main() -> I32 = answer
